{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-9378" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =8,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-9378" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =8.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-12{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-12{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=-12,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-12}{-2}=6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-9378" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5422e6b395a8e0ef352effcb47690917064e1225e5fdf2fbe777caeda3f7f381"} {"question" "q-9378" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-9378" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-9378" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-9379" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-9379" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =1.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}↔{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n6{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{3}{1}=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-9379" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5422e6b395a8e0ef352effcb47690917064e1225e5fdf2fbe777caeda3f7f381"} {"question" "q-9379" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-9379" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-9379" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-107109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 20 & 13\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-107109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-2.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 20 & 13\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 20 & 15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 20{x}_{1}+15{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 20 & 15 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{4}{-3}=-\\frac{4}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-107109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5422e6b395a8e0ef352effcb47690917064e1225e5fdf2fbe777caeda3f7f381"} {"question" "q-107109" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-107109" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "-\\frac{4}{3}"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-107109" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{4}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-18078" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-18078" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =1.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=6,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{6}{-2}=-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-18078" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5422e6b395a8e0ef352effcb47690917064e1225e5fdf2fbe777caeda3f7f381"} {"question" "q-18078" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-18078" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-18078" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-108039" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-108039" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =7.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{1}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-108039" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5422e6b395a8e0ef352effcb47690917064e1225e5fdf2fbe777caeda3f7f381"} {"question" "q-108039" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-108039" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-108039" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-80319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =10.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-80319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =10.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -6{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-6{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-80319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5422e6b395a8e0ef352effcb47690917064e1225e5fdf2fbe777caeda3f7f381"} {"question" "q-80319" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-80319" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-80319" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-80318" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-80318" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =6.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-5{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}={x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-80318" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5422e6b395a8e0ef352effcb47690917064e1225e5fdf2fbe777caeda3f7f381"} {"question" "q-80318" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-80318" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-80318" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-107273" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}22 & -40 \\\\ -5 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-107273" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}22 & -40 \\\\ -5 & 5\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}25 & -40 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}25{x}_{1}-40{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}25 & -40 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}25 & -40 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n25{x}_{1}-40{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{8}{5}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-107273" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5422e6b395a8e0ef352effcb47690917064e1225e5fdf2fbe777caeda3f7f381"} {"question" "q-107273" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-107273" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 40\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 25\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-107273" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-107140" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}17 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-107140" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =5.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}17 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}12{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}12 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n12{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-107140" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5422e6b395a8e0ef352effcb47690917064e1225e5fdf2fbe777caeda3f7f381"} {"question" "q-107140" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-107140" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-107140" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-107138" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-107138" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}8{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n8{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-107138" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5422e6b395a8e0ef352effcb47690917064e1225e5fdf2fbe777caeda3f7f381"} {"question" "q-107138" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-107138" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-107138" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-80324" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $\\lambda =2$ and $\\lambda =-1$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-80324" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{5}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-80324" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5422e6b395a8e0ef352effcb47690917064e1225e5fdf2fbe777caeda3f7f381"} {"question" "q-80324" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-80324" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-80324" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-9385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda =10$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-9385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-8{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-9385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5422e6b395a8e0ef352effcb47690917064e1225e5fdf2fbe777caeda3f7f381"} {"question" "q-9385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-9385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-9385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-9383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\lambda =4$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-9383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-9383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5422e6b395a8e0ef352effcb47690917064e1225e5fdf2fbe777caeda3f7f381"} {"question" "q-9383" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-9383" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-9383" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-107311" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $\\lambda =7$ and $\\lambda =29$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}11 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 25\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-107311" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}11 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 25\\end{aligned}]-7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 18\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -12{x}_{1}+18{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 18 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =29,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}11 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 25\\end{aligned}]-29[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-18 & -6 \\\\ -12 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-18{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -12{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-18 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -12 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-18 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-107311" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5422e6b395a8e0ef352effcb47690917064e1225e5fdf2fbe777caeda3f7f381"} {"question" "q-107311" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-107311" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-107311" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-80327" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $\\lambda =1$ and $\\lambda =10$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-80327" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 6 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-80327" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5422e6b395a8e0ef352effcb47690917064e1225e5fdf2fbe777caeda3f7f381"} {"question" "q-80327" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-80327" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "q-80327" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a46538cf9796ea80067126df1845bb2223bd58f1c84f516fdc9b0885a54d8e0"} {"question" "e-5484" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/c3f5f7169fd10d5710a95d0eab9c290faa04a544ac646a2ebcd24aa6b7e5f714.png) is an eigenvector of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/69c5e6ae96062d7c8e289e1f0070ab3874b15d0b42bb9300a50b9315723cfd8f.png)\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\,\\lambda=1$, then what is the value of $\\,\\dfrac{b}{a}\\,?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/example-5484.json" "file_sha256" "368877b7afb3ecddaa4add190d139b7bab381184445bd8e3cfc9299469be257f"} {"question" "e-5484" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\nx_1+5x_2=0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx_1=-5x_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/f8c4aba8e8fb922f08741deb8df0fe4f47c64c29b647fec7da2b0ce1b4367225.png)\n\nSetting, for instance, $x_2=-1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/5f7d229b6636bbfbe8eab4bdb7a6b8e72bb21c9cc9e66b1b4d607fd0e5e352e0.png)\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{b}{a}=\\dfrac{-1}{5}=-\\dfrac{1}{5}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/example-5484.json" "file_sha256" "368877b7afb3ecddaa4add190d139b7bab381184445bd8e3cfc9299469be257f"} {"question" "e-5483" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find an eigenvector of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/a37420b7df40cfb806fadac008680a2e7346b1b2dea5d89a4baa912ab0bedb14.png)\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\,\\lambda=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/example-5483.json" "file_sha256" "651b3a79c88f216118f69d61027e6d042db8c2376a51a4ffb0e5f253dcb1da5d"} {"question" "e-5483" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =2.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n5x_1-4x_2=0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx_1=\\dfrac{4}{5}x_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/65b8ab3a3e0506afb1a0c3a7a928e21b98bcda313bafcbcfdb5b02771dd91693.png)\n\nFinally, setting $x_2=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/a5098e5100bed7d15053e81b1127a774b828e30473718c6fc3fe73444eca3148.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/example-5483.json" "file_sha256" "651b3a79c88f216118f69d61027e6d042db8c2376a51a4ffb0e5f253dcb1da5d"} {"question" "e-5485" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $\\lambda=1$ and $\\lambda=-4$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/10510533350f1e77e4918cb4aff9edafe5ced005efc845becc8c1558bdd62811.png)\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/example-5485.json" "file_sha256" "8be84e67edca9c27483ca1d10565ceee6d0bee6c921a027b28c43321cc6191c8"} {"question" "e-5485" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can get two linearly independent eigenvectors by simply finding eigenvectors corresponding to the different eigenvalues.\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have to solve the corresponding system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $2$nd one). So, $x_1$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  $x_2=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/97e9cc73b2d39b924618c1372bf680ebf7d4374d1cf4fa9c3bc7e9afc05b06ea.png)\nSetting, for instance, $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/a01037d3f9d9e090010a555fa0e14d6bce72da48c59bd74b0f7dad6afcee71ba.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda =-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]-(-4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have to solve the corresponding system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain $x_1=\\dfrac{1}{5}x_2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/34313b24e58011d8be4790d57cd57d4800944903ff16dd20068b45bab2a7d261.png)\nSetting, for instance, $x_2=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/cb7300ef2064945937c27944e51032ade506ea16008e22f1a6ab54c5c647812c.png)\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/f6638f602a661865b1940f1409a20611c08f38821e2abcb204a4b38dd3a7adaa.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/example-5485.json" "file_sha256" "8be84e67edca9c27483ca1d10565ceee6d0bee6c921a027b28c43321cc6191c8"}] "usage" {"e-5483" [] "e-5484" [] "e-5485" []} "basis" 2349 "retractions" [[17592186092026 :question/problem "If $\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\,\\lambda=1$, then what is the value of $\\,\\dfrac{b}{a}\\,$?"] [17592186092026 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\,\\lambda =1\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - λI &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{x_{1} + 5x_{2} = 0; - x_{1} - 5x_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2}:=R_{2} + R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable.\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_1+5x_2=0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_1=-5x_2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-5x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, $x_2=-1$, we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{b}{a}=\\dfrac{-1}{5}=-\\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$"] [17592186092025 :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\,\\lambda=2$."] [17592186092025 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\,\\lambda =2\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - λI &= \\begin{bmatrix}7 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix} - 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{5x_{1} - 4x_{2} = 0; 5x_{1} - 4x_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-1)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable.\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n5x_1-4x_2=0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_1=\\dfrac{4}{5}x_2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5}x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting $x_2=5$, we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186092027 :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda=1$ and $\\lambda=-4$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A$."] [17592186092027 :question/worked-solution "We can get two linearly independent eigenvectors by simply finding eigenvectors corresponding to the different eigenvalues.\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately.\n\n- For $\\lambda =1$, we have\n$A - λI|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix} - 1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWe have to solve the corresponding system of linear equations:\n${-x_{2} = 0; - 5x_{2} = 0$\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n$M|= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-5)R_{1}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $2$nd one). So, $x_1$ is a free variable.\nFrom the first equation, we obtain $x_2=0$. Therefore, the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nSetting, for instance, $x_1=1$, we get the eigenvector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n- For $\\lambda =-4$, we have\n$A - λI|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix} - (-4)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWe have to solve the corresponding system of linear equations:\n${5x_{1} - x_{2} = 0; 0 = 0$\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n$M|= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nIn the matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable.\nFrom the first equation, we obtain $x_1=\\dfrac{1}{5}x_2$. Therefore, the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5}x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nSetting, for instance, $x_2=5$, we get the eigenvector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
